  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186083278,
      "id": "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e",
      "title": "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-9t,\\,y=-2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+12t,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Give exact answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/value": "-2",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/id": "a313c569-8d0a-56e9-946d-ba9ef6708637"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/id": "4fd63b9f-ed50-5f63-a1fa-00159e7948ce",
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "a313c569-8d0a-56e9-946d-ba9ef6708637"
          ]
        },
        {
          ":answer-field/key": "field-2",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/value": "1",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/id": "70727fab-d8c1-5733-a92b-d7958905ad0d"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/id": "617edfd5-96b3-5214-8097-550a0eebc917",
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "70727fab-d8c1-5733-a92b-d7958905ad0d"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if $y'(t)=0$ while $x'(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx'(t)={t}^{2}-9,\\qquad y'(t)=-6{t}^{2}-6t+12.\n$$\n\nSolving $y'(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{t}^{2}-6t+12 & =0 \\\\ -6(t-1)(t+2) & =0 \\\\ t & =-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(-2) & =-5\\ne 0, \\\\ x'(1) & =-8\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-2$ and $t=1$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/id": "900e8ba5-9647-5a27-9655-7749167178e2"
    },
    "generation": {
