    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-2t,\\,y=-{t}^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}+18t,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Give exact answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/value": "-3",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/id": "824f5db1-3e5f-585b-a0c0-e0d0cbd3d396"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/id": "0aae2fce-bbec-59d5-b0ba-89b7660c4b2e",
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "824f5db1-3e5f-585b-a0c0-e0d0cbd3d396"
          ]
        },
        {
          ":answer-field/key": "field-2",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/value": "2",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/id": "8d3f5964-b052-5319-ada2-7c9c6b243fbc"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/id": "8261d909-5391-5bc0-a076-4fe08f327d90",
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "8d3f5964-b052-5319-ada2-7c9c6b243fbc"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if $y'(t)=0$ while $x'(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=-{t}^{2}-3t-2,\\qquad y'(t)=-3{t}^{2}-3t+18.\n$$\n\nSolving $y'(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{t}^{2}-3t+18 & =0 \\\\ -3(t-2)(t+3) & =0 \\\\ t & =-3,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(-3) & =-2\\ne 0, \\\\ x'(2) & =-12\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-3$ and $t=2$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/id": "24b65f74-cd55-54f8-b5c0-57d815848a4b"
    },
