      ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if $y'(t)=0$ while $x'(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2{t}^{2}-2t-12,\\qquad y'(t)=-6{t}^{2}-24t-18.\n$$\n\nSolving $y'(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{t}^{2}-24t-18 & =0 \\\\ -6(t+1)(t+3) & =0 \\\\ t & =-3,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(-3) & =12\\ne 0, \\\\ x'(-1) & =-8\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-3$ and $t=-1$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/id": "17954ee1-f9c2-55a9-8c85-53ef2b5e512d"
    },
