      ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if $y'(t)=0$ while $x'(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=-{t}^{2}+2t+8,\\qquad y'(t)=3{t}^{2}-6t-9.\n$$\n\nSolving $y'(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}-6t-9 & =0 \\\\ 3(t-3)(t+1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-1,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(-1) & =5\\ne 0, \\\\ x'(3) & =5\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-1$ and $t=3$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/id": "667f08a5-3a20-5d8f-89af-b78b71b50ffb"
