      ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if $y'(t)=0$ while $x'(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=-2{t}^{2}-4t+16,\\qquad y'(t)=3{t}^{2}-21t+36.\n$$\n\nSolving $y'(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}-21t+36 & =0 \\\\ 3(t-4)(t-3) & =0 \\\\ t & =3,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(3) & =-14\\ne 0, \\\\ x'(4) & =-32\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=3$ and $t=4$.",
